answer 1: What happened: On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court granted review of whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) requires detention, during removal proceedings, of people who, like Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha, are in the United States and were not admitted. (`scotus-order-list-2026-10-01`; `scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:49: On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted. The order takes the case; it does not decide it. The detention statute deems a person present in the United States who has not been admitted an applicant for admission. The bond statute says the government "may release" a person on bond.
answer 2: Why it matters to someone who does not follow this subject: A person who has lived here for more than twenty years, with an asylum application and a work permit, was arrested on his way to work and later released on bond only after a judge found he was not a danger or a flight risk; if the government's reading wins, people in that situation can be held for the whole removal case without that hearing, and by the grant the dispute was no longer one man's case or the two appeals courts named in April. (`ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28`; `audit-gap-ca4-2026-09-10`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:61: Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. If the government wins, people in his situation cannot be released on bond under the detention statute. The Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he "must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond". The question the Court agreed to decide is whether the detention statute requires that detention. Whether the Constitution separately requires a hearing is not part of the question. The Fourth Circuit names eight courts, the First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh, that held the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the people who filed the cases, and that they must be given a bond hearing. On September 10, 2026, that court joined them. The Fifth and the Eighth held that the detention statute applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted, and that they must be detained without bond.
answer 3: The easiest wrong reading, the one the headline or the coverage invites: That the Court is deciding whether the administration may detain tens of thousands of people with no hearing at all, as a yes-or-no on a detention policy, and that the grant means the detention is now the law. (`cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01`; `scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:86: CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during removal proceedings without bond hearings. The October 1 order, the Supreme Court's docket for this case, the Second Circuit's opinion, the Board of Immigration Appeals decision, and the July 10 Customs memorandum do not give a precise count of people held under the government's reading. The Fourth Circuit says district courts sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases, which counts court cases, not people in detention.
answer 4: The one concept a stranger must hold to follow it, the mechanism: The detention statute says an applicant for admission shall be detained when an alien seeking admission is not clearly entitled to be admitted; the Second Circuit held that a man who entered years ago may be an applicant for admission and still not be seeking admission, so the bond statute applies, and that statute says the government may release him. (`ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28`; `uscode-2024-1225`; `uscode-2024-1226`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:57: The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization; he was already living here. So the court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Parole is a separate power. The parole statute lets the government release a person "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit". The July 10 memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted.
answer 5: What changed: A 1997 rule stated bond eligibility for people present without admission, and the Second Circuit describes that practice as thirty years old; in July 2025 the government said section 235, not the bond section, applies to all applicants for admission; the Board said judges lack authority to grant bond; the Second Circuit held the opposite on April 28 and on September 25 refused to rehear the case, with a dissent and a concurrence that agreed with the dissent's analysis; on October 1 the Supreme Court granted review. (`audit-gap-fr-1997-03-06`; `ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28`; `cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10`; `bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05`; `ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25`; `audit-gap-ca2-enbanc-complete`; `scotus-order-list-2026-10-01`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:103: On March 6, 1997, a Federal Register rule, effective April 1, said people present without having been admitted or paroled would be eligible for bond. On September 25, 2026, the full Second Circuit refused to rehear the case. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented, arguing that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted against rehearing while writing that he agreed with the dissent's analysis. He wrote that mere substantive disagreement with the panel opinion does not warrant the time and effort of full court rehearing.
answer 6: What is unresolved as of the newest dated record: As of the October 5 docket the Court has not decided the case and the October 1 argument list sets no day for this case; the question it accepted is the statute, not due process, which the government's petition says a companion case also presents; the text of the August 21 response was posted on October 5 and the September 4 reply is still listed without its text; other petitions on the same question were also at the Court. (`scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05`; `scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21`; `scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01`; `scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01`; `doj-petition-26-104`; `audit-gap-scotus-25-1415-26-43`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:127: No argument date has been set. The Court took one question, what the detention statute means. Whether holding someone without a bond hearing violates the Constitution's guarantee of due process is not part of it; the government's petition says a second case, Lopez-Campos from the Sixth Circuit, raises that question as well. The Court had not granted or denied review in that case or in a third, Buenrostro-Mendez from the Fifth Circuit, as of October 3.

