### immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r3-editor.md
## First read

The page told me that the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether people living here after entry without admission must be detained during removal proceedings without bond, after the government changed its reading and appeals courts split.

At the end, I wanted to know whether the grant changed anyone’s custody now, why the Court took this case despite the government’s request to wait, and where the related petitions stood. I first lost the thread at: “in the case of an applicant for admission, a person seeking admission ‘shall be detained’”.

## The story

The record supports this story in one sentence: a July 2025 detention reading displaced bond eligibility for people arrested inside the country years after entry, Barbosa da Cunha won a bond hearing and release, appeals courts split, and the Supreme Court granted review of the statutory question on October 1, 2026. The [1997 rule](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt) says this group “will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination”; the [July memorandum](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt) assigns “section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236”; the [order](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt) says “The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.”

**Nothing later displaces the grant in the checked primary record.** The [current case docket](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-104.html) ends its proceedings with “Petition GRANTED.” and an October 1 filing instruction. The [Lopez-Campos](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-1415.html) and [Buenrostro-Mendez](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-43.html) dockets show no later disposition. The [ACLU’s October 1 statement](https://www.aclu.org/press-releases/aclu-comment-on-supreme-court-agreeing-to-hear-challenge-over-trump-mandatory-detention-policy), checked as the actor’s primary statement behind coverage, reports the grant and reaction, not a later court act.

The page has the right angle and opening act. One passage near the top dates the changed position, denied hearing, release, appellate ruling, and grant, so a reader need not reconstruct that sequence. What remains unresolved is also stated. The page differs from the best telling chiefly in **when** it asks readers to parse the statute: exceptions arrive before the person and consequence have made the distinction useful.

| Reader-model answer | Does the marked paragraph carry it? |
| --- | --- |
| 1. What happened | **Yes.** It states the grant, question, and that the order decides nothing yet. |
| 2. Why it matters | **Yes.** It gives his circumstances, loss of bond under the government’s reading, and the wider split. The early sequence supplies the hearing and release. |
| 3. Easiest wrong reading | **Partly.** The marked paragraph distinguishes cases from people detained; the narrower statutory question and grant-versus-ruling correction are elsewhere. Ask for the correction in a sentence beside the CBS framing, using copy already on the page. |
| 4. Mechanism | **Yes, with effort.** It explains why the Second Circuit said he was not seeking admission and states the parole alternative. The bond statute’s power to release appears earlier. |
| 5. What changed | **No, as marked.** That paragraph gives the 1997 rule and rehearing dispute, while the early dated passage gives July, the Board, April, and the grant. The page carries the answer, but the mark sits on an afterword. Keep the early sequence as its sentence-level answer. |
| 6. Unresolved | **Yes.** It gives the unset argument date, statutory scope, due process limit, and status of the other petitions as of October 3. |

## The top

The headline survives the easy wrong reading: “agreed to decide” does not announce a detention ruling or endorse a population count. The first sentence says what happened, and no person is named before introduction. The dek gives the old practice and new position. The first paragraph then leans on “applicant for admission” and “seeking admission” before explaining their difference, and spends 75 words on the statutory clause and exceptions. **The top fails a cold reader there**, despite an accurate headline and first sentence.

