## First read

The page told me that the Supreme Court will decide whether people arrested inside the country years after entering without admission can be denied bond while their removal cases proceed.

At the end, I wanted to know when the Court would hear the case and whether the related petitions had moved. I first lost the thread at: “Before 1996, the procedures for detaining and expelling a person depended largely on whether the person had physically entered the United States.” These are my notes from reading the [story view](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-05-r7c-editor-page.txt), before opening the reader model.

## The story

The record supports a story about a July 2025 reversal of bond practice, a man denied a hearing after an arrest on his way to work, opposing appeals rulings, and the Supreme Court’s October 1 grant on the detention statute. The [1997 rule](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt:203) made this group “eligible for bond and bond redetermination”; the [Customs memorandum](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt) assigned applicants for admission to “section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236”; and the [October 1 order](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt) states, “The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.”

The page has the right angle and opening act. Its second story paragraph gives the 2025 change, denied hearing, bond release and April ruling in date order, with the October 1 grant immediately above it. What changed is clear. What remains unresolved is stated, though the closing status stops at October 3 under an October 5 update stamp. The live [Rhoney](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-104.html), [Lopez-Campos](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-1415.html) and [Buenrostro-Mendez](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-43.html) dockets showed no later disposition when checked for this memo. Rhoney still had no argument date there.

| Reader-model answer | Editorial call |
| --- | --- |
| 1. What happened | **Yes.** The marked paragraph states the grant and says it is not a decision. |
| 2. Why it matters | **Yes, late.** The marked paragraph gives the man’s circumstances, the loss of bond under the government’s reading, and the split. Its long circuit sentence makes the reach hard to repeat. |
| 3. Easiest wrong reading | **Yes.** The page separates a grant from a ruling and cases brought from people detained. The list of five records makes that correction laborious. |
| 4. Mechanism | **Partly in a cold read.** The marked paragraph gives the Second Circuit’s distinction and the parole alternative. The meaning of admission arrives after the headline and opening. Move the existing definition forward. |
| 5. What changed | **Yes across the story, not in its marked paragraph alone.** The early sequence carries July 2025, the Board and the April ruling; the marked paragraph adds the 1997 rule and September rehearing. Do not add another chronology. |
| 6. What is unresolved | **Yes, with a dated limit.** No argument date and the separation of the statutory and due process questions are repeatable. Bring the related petitions’ status up to the page’s update date. |

## The stranger’s read

I take the [stranger report](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-05-r7c-stranger.md) in its order. A leave call means no additional finding.

| Item, in report order | Call |
| --- | --- |
| What happened | **Leave:** the grant is clear. |
| Why it matters | **Act, edit 7:** make the split easier to retain; the arrest and bond result are already early. |
| Wrong reading | **Act, edit 8:** shorten the record inventory while preserving the count distinction. |
| Mechanism | **Act, edit 1 and hole 1:** put admission’s meaning where it is first needed. |
| What changed | **Leave:** the early dated sequence carries it. |
| Unresolved | **Act, edit 10:** reconcile the status date with the update stamp. |
| Next questions | **Act, edit 7:** untangle the circuit sentence. |
| Paroled and entered without inspection | **Act, edit 2:** reduce the qualifications in the first practice sentence. This is a residual stall after the ledger’s earlier opening patch. |
| The government’s petition and that disparity | **Act, edit 3:** identify the filing and the two sides of the disparity. |
| Barbosa da Cunha’s response | **Act, edit 4:** say what filing it answers. |
| That court joined them; Fifth and Eighth | **Act, edit 7:** name the Fourth Circuit and the result. |
| People who filed the cases | **Act, edit 7:** remove the interruption from the split sentence. |
| That case in the closing paragraph | **Act, edit 10:** name Lopez-Campos there. |
| October 3 beneath an October 5 stamp | **Act, edit 10.** |
| Bare Barbosa da Cunha beneath the quote card | **Leave:** it labels the person whose opinion excerpt the card displays. |
| Complete rehearing file and differently saved dockets | **Leave:** these are source-list labels, not story claims. |
| Petitioners in the case count | **Leave:** the sentence immediately distinguishes court cases from people detained; the older stall has no new patch to assess. |
